  <!-- ================= WHAT BUYERS GET WRONG ================= -->
  <section class="py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 article-body">
      <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-orange mb-3">First order</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-navy leading-tight mb-6">
        What do buyers get wrong on a first order?
      </h2>
      <p>
        Five mistakes account for most of the disappointing first shipments we see, and none of them is about the product.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Ordering to the price tier instead of the container.</strong> A quantity break saves a few percent on the
          goods; filling the container saves far more on the freight per piece. The container wins almost every time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Approving a sample without checking the carton.</strong> The carton is what you pay freight on, and it is
          the line almost nobody verifies. Measure it, and compare it with the quotation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Treating a toy certificate as a cosmetic file.</strong> A makeup set that goes on the skin is a cosmetic
          in most markets. The toy report answers a different question, and a pharmacy buyer will spot the gap immediately.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Counting forwards from the order instead of backwards from the shelf.</strong> Production, sea freight,
          clearance and inland delivery together are ten to twelve weeks. A Q4 shelf date means a summer purchase order,
          not an autumn one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Skipping the pre-shipment inspection to save a few hundred dollars.</strong> The inspection is the last
          point at which a defect is cheap to fix. After the container sails, the return freight usually exceeds the value
          of the claim — which is exactly why this is B2B wholesale with no unconditional returns.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each of those is a decision you can make correctly with no extra cost, just a week earlier. If you want the
        arithmetic behind them, the <a href="/case-studies/" class="text-orange font-semibold">order walk-throughs</a> show
        both a mixed first container and a private-label programme with the numbers visible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

